POSTS={
"pinellas-flood-zones-explained":{
"title":"Pinellas County Flood Zones Explained",
"desc":"Flood zones decide your insurance, your lender and what you can rebuild. Here is how Pinellas zones work and how to find yours.",
"h1":"Pinellas County Flood Zones Explained for Homeowners",
"eyebrow":"Local conditions","img":"flooded-storage-room-standing-water-largo.jpg",
"intro":[
 "Flood zones in Pinellas County decide more than your insurance premium. They affect what a lender requires, what the county can require after a major repair, and how much risk your property actually carries during a storm.",
 "Two houses on the same street can sit in different zones. Elevation here changes over short distances, so the only answer that matters is the one for your own parcel."],
"sections":[
 ("The Zones You Will Actually See",[
  ("table",["Zone","What it means","Insurance effect"],
   [["AE","High risk, with a published base flood elevation","Flood insurance normally required by lenders"],
    ["A","High risk, no detailed elevation study","Required by lenders, rating uses other methods"],
    ["VE","High risk coastal with wave action","Highest premiums, strictest building rules"],
    ["X shaded","Moderate risk","Not usually required, still worth carrying"],
    ["X","Minimal risk","Not required, optional and inexpensive"]]),
  "A large share of claims come from zone X properties, because owners there often carry no flood policy at all."]),
 ("Base Flood Elevation and Why It Drives Everything",[
  "Base flood elevation is the height floodwater is expected to reach in a one percent annual chance event. Your finished floor elevation is compared against it.",
  "A floor above base flood elevation rates very differently from one below it. An elevation certificate documents that comparison and is worth obtaining before you shop for coverage."]),
 ("The Fifty Percent Rule",[
  "If you make substantial improvements to a property in a high risk zone, or repair damage where the cost reaches roughly half the structure's market value, local rules can require the whole building to be brought into compliance with current flood standards.",
  "In practice that can mean elevating the structure. This is why owners of older beach and low lying properties should understand the rule before a large repair, not during one."]),
 ("Where Risk Concentrates in Pinellas",[
  ("ul",["Barrier islands including Indian Rocks Beach and Madeira Beach",
         "Low lying mainland neighbourhoods such as Shore Acres",
         "Canal front properties on Boca Ciega Bay",
         "Parts of Pinellas Park with a high water table",
         "Properties draining toward creeks and bayous"])]),
 ("How to Find Your Zone",[
  "Use the FEMA Flood Map Service Center or the Pinellas County property information portal and search your address. Both will return the zone designation for your parcel.",
  "Check the map date as well. Zones are revised, and a property can move between designations when maps are updated."]),
 ("What Zone Does Not Tell You",[
  "A favourable zone is not a guarantee. Mapped zones reflect modelled risk, and water does not read maps. Street flooding, drainage failures and unusually heavy rain all produce losses outside mapped high risk areas.",
  "The useful view is that zone sets your requirement and your premium, while elevation and drainage set your actual exposure."])],
"guides_h2":"Related Local and Prevention Guides",
"svcblurbs":["Muck out, sanitizing, flood cuts and structural drying after surge water.",
 "Board up, tarping, water removal and rebuild after wind and rain."],
"faq_h2":"Pinellas Flood Zone Questions",
"faqs":[
 ("How do I find my flood zone in Pinellas County?","Search your address on the FEMA Flood Map Service Center or the Pinellas County property portal. Both return the zone designation for your parcel."),
 ("What is the difference between zone AE and zone X?","AE is high risk with a published base flood elevation and usually requires flood insurance. X is minimal risk where coverage is optional but still worth carrying."),
 ("Do I need flood insurance in zone X?","It is not normally required, but a meaningful share of flood claims come from zone X properties. Coverage there is usually inexpensive."),
 ("What is base flood elevation?","The height floodwater is expected to reach in a one percent annual chance event. Your finished floor height is compared against it for rating."),
 ("What is an elevation certificate?","A surveyed document showing how your finished floor compares to base flood elevation. It affects your premium and what can be required after major repairs."),
 ("What is the fifty percent rule?","If repair or improvement costs approach half the structure's market value in a high risk zone, local rules can require the building to meet current flood standards."),
 ("Can my flood zone change?","Yes. Maps are revised periodically and a property can move between designations, which changes both requirements and premiums."),
 ("Does a high zone mean my house will flood?","No. Zones reflect modelled risk. Your actual exposure depends on your elevation, local drainage and the particular storm."),
 ("Does homeowners insurance cover any flooding?","No. Water rising from outside the structure is excluded and requires a separate flood policy regardless of the zone."),
 ("Should I buy flood insurance during hurricane season?","Buy before it. New flood policies commonly carry a waiting period, so coverage arranged once a storm is named usually does not apply.")]},

"is-it-safe-to-stay-during-water-damage":{
"title":"Is It Safe to Stay Home During Water Damage?",
"desc":"Most clean water losses do not require leaving. Sewage, surge water and wet electrics change the answer. Here is how to decide.",
"h1":"Is It Safe to Stay in Your Home During Water Damage",
"eyebrow":"Emergency response","img":"dehumidifier-kitchen-water-damage-largo.jpg",
"intro":[
 "Most Largo homeowners can stay in the house during water damage work, and in a contained clean water loss there is usually no reason to leave. The answer changes when the water was contaminated, when the electrical system was involved, or when the affected area includes the only bathroom or kitchen.",
 "The decision comes down to three things: what kind of water it was, which parts of the house are affected, and who lives there."],
"sections":[
 ("The Three Water Categories",[
  ("table",["Category","Source","Staying in the home"],
   [["Category 1","Clean supply line, water heater, rain","Usually fine"],
    ["Category 2","Appliance discharge, aquarium","Usually fine with the area contained"],
    ["Category 3","Sewage, toilet past the trap, surge or flood water","Stay out of the affected area, often stay elsewhere"]])]),
 ("When Leaving Is the Safer Call",[
  ("ul",["Sewage or flood water reached living space",
         "The electrical panel or circuits were submerged and power is off",
         "A ceiling is sagging and has not yet been taken down",
         "The only bathroom or kitchen is inside the work area",
         "Someone in the house has asthma, a compromised immune system, or is recovering from surgery",
         "Containment for mold removal covers a route you need to use daily"])]),
 ("Living Alongside Drying Equipment",[
  "Air movers produce steady noise comparable to a strong fan, and the equipment runs continuously, including overnight. Most people adjust within a day.",
  "Dehumidifiers make rooms warmer and drier than usual. Doors into the work area stay closed to keep the drying chamber sealed, which changes how you move around the house.",
  "Ask for placement to be adjusted around bedrooms if sleep is a problem. What cannot be done is switching equipment off at night, because drying is cumulative and shutdowns add days."]),
 ("Children and Pets",[
  "Keep both out of work areas. Equipment has exposed cords and intake grilles, containment sheeting is not a barrier a child should test, and material being removed may have sharp edges.",
  "Cats and small dogs will find their way behind plastic sheeting if there is any gap, so a closed door is more reliable than tape."]),
 ("What to Ask Before Deciding",[
  ("ul",["What category was the water?",
         "Which rooms are inside the containment area?",
         "Is the electrical system in those rooms safe to energise?",
         "How many days will equipment run?",
         "Will any demolition produce dust in living space?"])]),
 ("Insurance and Additional Living Expense",[
  "Many homeowners policies include additional living expense coverage, which pays for temporary accommodation when the home is not reasonably habitable.",
  "That phrase matters. If your crew advises leaving and the reason is documented, that record supports the claim. Ask your adjuster before booking rather than after."])],
"guides_h2":"More Emergency Response Guides",
"svcblurbs":["Extraction for standing water before drying equipment goes in.",
 "Muck out and sanitizing after contaminated storm or surge water."],
"faq_h2":"Staying Home During Water Damage: Common Questions",
"faqs":[
 ("Do I have to move out during water damage repairs?","Usually not for a contained clean water loss. Sewage, surge water in living space or a submerged electrical system are the cases where staying elsewhere is safer."),
 ("Is drying equipment dangerous to be around?","No, but keep children and pets away from cords and intake grilles, and keep the work area doors closed so the drying chamber stays sealed."),
 ("Can I sleep in the house with equipment running?","Most people do. Air movers are about as loud as a strong fan, and placement can usually be adjusted around bedrooms."),
 ("Can the equipment be turned off at night?","No. Drying is cumulative and every shutdown lets material reabsorb moisture, which extends the job by days."),
 ("Is mold remediation safe to stay through?","Usually, with containment and negative air in place. People with asthma, allergies or immune conditions often prefer to stay elsewhere during removal."),
 ("What if sewage reached my living room?","Stay out of the affected area. Category three water carries pathogens, and porous material that absorbed it is removed rather than cleaned in place."),
 ("Will my insurance pay for a hotel?","Many policies include additional living expense coverage when the home is not reasonably habitable. Ask your adjuster before booking."),
 ("Is the house safe once the water is gone?","Not necessarily. Wet electrical components, a sagging ceiling and contaminated material all remain hazards after standing water is removed."),
 ("Should I be worried about air quality during drying?","Clean water drying does not usually affect air quality. Where mold is present, containment and HEPA filtration keep the work area separate."),
 ("How many days will the equipment be here?","Most residential losses run three to five days. Block walls, tile over slab and hardwood extend that.")]},

"cast-iron-drain-failure-largo":{
"title":"Cast Iron Drain Failure in Largo Slab Homes",
"desc":"Repeat backups in a 1960s Largo home usually mean the cast iron drain under the slab has failed. Here is how to confirm it.",
"h1":"Cast Iron Drain Failure Under Largo Slab Homes",
"eyebrow":"Sources of water damage","img":"flooded-storage-room-standing-water-largo.jpg",
"intro":[
 "Cast iron drain failure is one of the most common hidden problems in Largo homes built between the 1950s and the 1970s. The lines run under the slab, they corrode from the inside, and by the time symptoms reach the surface the pipe has usually been failing for years.",
 "The giveaway is repetition. A one off clog is a clog. The same fixture backing up repeatedly in a house of that age is usually the pipe itself."],
"sections":[
 ("How Cast Iron Fails",[
  "Cast iron corrodes from the inside out. Waste and water erode the pipe wall along the bottom, where flow sits longest, until the channel becomes rough, then scaled, then holed.",
  "Roots find those holes. Soil washes in. The line sags where support is lost, which creates a belly that holds standing water and accelerates everything.",
  ("table",["Stage","What you notice"],
   [["Scaling","Slower drainage, occasional gurgle"],
    ["Channelling","Repeat clogs in the same fixture"],
    ["Holing and root intrusion","Frequent backups, smell, soft spots in the yard"],
    ["Collapse","Standing sewage, drains that stop working entirely"]])]),
 ("Why It Matters More Under a Slab",[
  "There is no crawl space to inspect and no easy access. Failures happen under concrete, and when the line finally fails water and waste either back up into the house or soak the soil under the slab.",
  "Soil washing out from under a slab is the serious version. It causes settlement, and settlement produces cracks you will notice in the walls long before anyone blames the drain."]),
 ("The Signs Worth Acting On",[
  ("ul",["The same fixture backing up more than once",
         "Gurgling from a tub or toilet when another fixture drains",
         "A sewage smell indoors or in the yard with no obvious source",
         "Patches of unusually green or sunken grass over the line route",
         "Cracking in floor tile along a straight line",
         "Slow drainage across several fixtures at once"])]),
 ("Camera Inspection Settles It",[
  "A plumber runs a camera down the line and records the condition. That recording shows whether you have a clog, a belly, root intrusion or a failed section, and it identifies the footage where the problem sits.",
  "It is worth doing after a second backup rather than a fifth. Each backup that reaches the floor is a category three water loss in your living space."]),
 ("Repair Options",[
  ("table",["Approach","What it involves"],
   [["Spot repair","Open the slab at one point and replace a failed section"],
    ["Pipe lining","A resin liner cured in place inside the existing pipe, less demolition"],
    ["Full replacement","Open trenches through the slab and replace the drain system"],
    ["Reroute","Abandon the under slab line and run new drainage overhead or around"]]),
  "Which one applies depends on how much of the line has failed, which is exactly what the camera inspection establishes."]),
 ("What Happens on the Water Damage Side",[
  "A backup through a floor drain or toilet is category three water. Carpet, pad and affected porous material are removed rather than dried, surfaces are cleaned and sanitised, and the structure is dried and verified afterward.",
  "The plumbing repair and the restoration are separate jobs. Stopping the source comes first, because drying a space that is going to back up again achieves nothing."])],
"guides_h2":"Related Guides on Plumbing Failures",
"svcblurbs":["Extraction, cavity drying and repair after a supply or drain failure.",
 "Drying after appliance, water heater and connection failures."],
"faq_h2":"Cast Iron Drain Questions From Largo Owners",
"faqs":[
 ("How long does cast iron drain pipe last?","Commonly fifty to seventy years in service. Homes built in Largo in the 1950s and 1960s are at or past that range if the lines were never replaced."),
 ("Why does the same drain keep backing up?","In an older slab home a repeat backup in one fixture usually means the line itself has channelled or collapsed rather than that something was flushed."),
 ("How do I confirm a failed drain line?","A camera inspection run by a plumber records the pipe's condition and locates the failure by footage. It is the only way to be certain without opening the slab."),
 ("Can a failed drain damage my foundation?","It can. Water escaping under a slab washes out supporting soil, which causes settlement and the wall and tile cracking that follows."),
 ("Is cast iron replacement covered by insurance?","The pipe itself is generally treated as maintenance and not covered. Resulting sudden water damage inside the home may be, depending on the policy and the cause."),
 ("What is pipe lining?","A resin liner installed inside the existing pipe and cured in place. It restores a smooth channel with far less demolition than trenching the slab."),
 ("Is a sewage backup dangerous?","Yes. It is category three water carrying pathogens, so affected porous material is removed rather than cleaned and the area is sanitised."),
 ("Why does my yard smell of sewage?","A failed under slab or lateral line can release waste into the soil. Unusually green or sunken grass over the pipe route often appears with it."),
 ("Should I replace the whole line or just one section?","That depends on how much has failed, which the camera inspection shows. Spot repairs make sense on an otherwise sound line and not on a deteriorated one."),
 ("Who is responsible, me or the utility?","A failure in your lateral is generally the property owner's. A main line failure is the utility's. Documenting the source is what settles it.")]},
}
